# yardr.train: the flow of depot yardr's trains (yardr depot flow yardr --type train
# yardr.train). A train waits in open while its children are open (each child
# blocks it); once the last closes, yardr-integrators rebases its branch onto
# main and tests it, Codex reviews the whole diff, another agent reviews the
# whole of it again for security alone (the wagons among it, which nobody
# reviewed one by one), and the train lands on main locally, where the assembly
# fast-forwards main and advances it straight to merged (the bare edge, which no
# outcome offers), or, experimentally, through a pull request (approved ->
# in-pr -> land): only pr-train reports done in approved, and without
# YARDR_PR=1 in its cmd it holds the train instead, as pr-merge does in land. A
# rebase onto main that conflicts, in open or in approved, goes to the mergers;
# a resolved train goes back to open, where it is integrated (and reviewed)
# again.
#
# No session reports merged, and none reports changes in approved: those are
# what GitHub's events about the train's pull request say, through the table
# on the yard's github hook (yardr hook show gh). A pull request merged is
# merged wherever the train waits on it, changes requested send it back to
# open, one closed unmerged is a question, and CI read green in in-pr is
# done. See docs/line.md#trains.
backlog -> open
open -> review on done | open on failed | merge on conflict | backlog
review -> review-security on done | open on changes | decide on question
review-security -> approved on done | open on changes | decide on question
approved -> in-pr on done | open on failed | merge on conflict | open on changes | merged on merged | merged
in-pr -> land on done | open on changes | decide on question | merged on merged
land -> merged on done | open on failed | merged on merged
merge -> open on done | open on rework | decide on question
decide -> open | backlog
merged terminal
